00:11:21.104 --> 00:11:25.135
<v Host>Time for a short quiz. Listen and try to answer.

00:11:25.735 --> 00:11:36.655
<v Slow>8月1日からは、「マイナ保険証」か、もう一つ、何という書類が必要ですか。

00:11:37.456 --> 00:11:44.943
<v Host>The eligibility confirmation certificate — a document sent free of charge to people without the digital card.

00:11:45.543 --> 00:11:56.224
<v Slow>5月に病院などに行った人のうち、マイナ保険証を使った人は、何パーセントでしたか。

00:11:57.024 --> 00:11:59.159
<v Host>Sixty-eight point five percent.

00:11:59.759 --> 00:12:27.000
<v Host>That's all for today. As of August first, Japan's paper health insurance cards are gone for good. At the hospital you now need the digital insurance card or the eligibility certificate, and people with neither must pay the full cost up front. Only about sixty-eight percent of patients were actually using the digital card in May, so the government is urging everyone to check what they have before they get sick. Thanks for listening, and see you tomorrow.

00:12:27.600 --> 00:12:30.000
<v News>また明日。
